00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:50.760
So if you don't know
LL Bean, LL Bean

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:56.640
is a company that sells apparel
and equipment for, well,

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:59.870
either casual wear or outdoors.

00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:04.680
You might have seen our boots
or have a pair of our boots.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:06.210
And our catalogs
have been around

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:07.780
in the mail for a long time.

00:02:10.509 --> 00:02:14.110
LL Bean is a
family-owned company.

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:16.870
It's been in
business since 1912,

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:19.750
when we started out
as a catalog company.

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:26.230
LL Bean is passionate
about getting people

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:27.760
into the outdoors.

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:31.000
And our brand strategy
is really about providing

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:33.250
products and
experiences that are

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:35.980
made for the sheer
joy of the outdoors.

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:39.950
Recently, we just launched
our Be an Outsider campaign.

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:41.470
You might have seen that.

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:46.330
And that's really about
getting families outside.

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:49.030
And family is whatever
you consider your family.

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:53.650
It might be your kids,
your pets, or whatever.

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:56.650
And then also we've
extended that-- you

00:02:56.650 --> 00:02:59.230
may have seen a little bit
of that-- into the workplace,

00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:02.590
with our Be an Outsider at
Work, where we partnered

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:06.999
with Industrious to deliver
some outside work spaces

00:03:06.999 --> 00:03:08.290
in and around different cities.

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:17.240
So like I mentioned, LL Bean
started out as a catalog

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:19.580
company back in 1912.

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:24.560
And catalog is what really drove
our business for the first part

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:27.520
of the 20th century.

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:32.890
In 1996, we launched
our e-commerce website.

00:03:32.890 --> 00:03:38.430
It was pretty early
on in that era.

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:41.640
And today, our
e-commerce website

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:47.460
actually accounts for more
than 2/3 of our revenue.

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:52.140
Other than the catalog,
up until the early 2000s,

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:54.060
we really had just
one retail store

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:55.500
located in Freeport,
Maine, where

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:58.200
the company headquarters are.

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:01.050
And in early 2000s
or so, we started

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:04.110
to expand our retail
presence a bit.

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:11.130
And today, we've got over 50
stores around the country,

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:17.230
and kiosks and some other
mobile stores as well.

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:25.160
As we kind of went
through the change

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:31.340
on how we were delivering
capabilities to our customers

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:35.630
and doing business, each
channel sort of kind of

00:04:35.630 --> 00:04:37.260
grew up on its own.

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:40.490
So we had systems to really
support that direct marketing

00:04:40.490 --> 00:04:43.560
and catalog and, you
know, phone order entries

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:45.110
and those kinds of things.

00:04:45.110 --> 00:04:47.480
When we launched the
website, we had a website

00:04:47.480 --> 00:04:50.600
that had a lot of
the capabilities

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:52.430
for taking orders over the web.

00:04:52.430 --> 00:04:54.290
And then store sales.

00:04:54.290 --> 00:04:59.030
Part of the issue is
that as time's gone on

00:04:59.030 --> 00:05:01.250
and customers have
started experiencing us

00:05:01.250 --> 00:05:04.640
across these different
channels, their interactions

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:05.760
are changing as well.

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:11.900
They want to do things and
interact with LL Bean in all

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:13.400
of those different channels.

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:15.860
And they want to
do it seamlessly.

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:20.570
And part of the problem was
is that our systems had really

00:05:20.570 --> 00:05:24.440
kind of reached the point where
they just couldn't support

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:26.765
those evolving customer needs.

00:05:31.507 --> 00:05:33.090
CHRIS CUMMINGS: OK,
we're going to get

00:05:33.090 --> 00:05:34.870
to the what we did here soon.

00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:38.190
But one thing first is just
to highlight that this is not

00:05:38.190 --> 00:05:40.350
a unique to LL Bean trend.

00:05:40.350 --> 00:05:42.640
It's not even a unique
to retail trend.

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:45.540
There are customers across
all kinds of verticals

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:47.790
are changing their behavior.

00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:52.160
They have different
expectations of brands.

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:53.910
And there's all kinds
of metrics out there

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:55.860
in the industry
to reference that.

00:05:55.860 --> 00:05:58.200
We pulled three of
them up here to share.

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:02.400
The first one, "Harvard Business
Review" says 73% of consumers

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:07.140
now use multiple channels when
making a purchase from a brand.

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:10.080
And 87% of the
customers surveyed

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:12.780
think that brands
need to invest more

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:15.810
in making those
experiences across channels

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:17.190
more consistent.

00:06:17.190 --> 00:06:20.580
So they're clearly saying
something to the brands

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:23.220
these days that
they're not happy.

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:25.620
The experiences
are not consistent.

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:27.169
They want the same
company no matter

00:06:27.169 --> 00:06:28.710
whether they're
interacting with them

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:32.250
on a web channel, on
their phone, in the store.

00:06:32.250 --> 00:06:34.710
And they don't want to start
a transaction in one channel

00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:37.770
and then start from
scratch in another.

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:40.890
So expectations have been
raised for the brands.

00:06:40.890 --> 00:06:42.130
Investment is required.

00:06:42.130 --> 00:06:45.870
But the good news is that
there is ROI available.

00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:49.350
Last metric is 30%
higher lifetime value

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:52.380
for customers interacting
through multiple channels

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:56.100
versus those who only engage
through a single channel.

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:58.610
And though the bar
has been raised,

00:06:58.610 --> 00:07:00.270
the brands are stepping up.

00:07:00.270 --> 00:07:02.370
They need to step up
and meet this challenge.

00:07:02.370 --> 00:07:05.370
And frankly, the way we've
all been building systems

00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:08.040
for the last two decades
is no longer good enough

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:09.122
to meet that challenge.

00:07:09.122 --> 00:07:10.455
Something clearly has to change.

00:07:13.750 --> 00:07:15.310
RANDY DYER: So about
three years ago,

00:07:15.310 --> 00:07:18.160
we were approached
and asked if we

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:22.360
could look at how we can
simplify the interactions

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:24.110
with our customers.

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:25.960
So one of the things
that we did is just

00:07:25.960 --> 00:07:29.020
took a step back
and looked at all

00:07:29.020 --> 00:07:33.500
of those different
interactions and saw

00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:35.500
that things like
a customer might

00:07:35.500 --> 00:07:38.860
come into a store with an item
that they purchased online,

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:40.480
and they want to
return it, and they

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:43.930
want to replace it with another
item that maybe doesn't exist

00:07:43.930 --> 00:07:44.530
in the store.

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:47.200
So that order is going
to be placed in the store

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:53.660
and shipped from the online
channel, as an example.

00:07:53.660 --> 00:07:57.850
So in looking at all of
those different interactions,

00:07:57.850 --> 00:08:00.910
we started to then step back and
say, what are the inhibitors?

00:08:00.910 --> 00:08:03.910
What's kind of keeping us
from delivering those cleaner

00:08:03.910 --> 00:08:06.180
customer experiences?

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:08.530
As I mentioned
earlier, our systems

00:08:08.530 --> 00:08:11.366
were a core part of
some of those barriers.

00:08:11.366 --> 00:08:13.240
Not that they didn't
have great capabilities,

00:08:13.240 --> 00:08:16.320
because in their own channels
and in their own right,

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:18.230
they do.

00:08:18.230 --> 00:08:21.100
But they just weren't
designed to work

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:25.940
with each other for these
new kind of interactions.

00:08:25.940 --> 00:08:32.890
So we stepped back and looked
at our application architecture

00:08:32.890 --> 00:08:35.350
to really understand
what was going on.

00:08:35.350 --> 00:08:39.580
And some of the things that
we found are somewhat obvious.

00:08:39.580 --> 00:08:41.890
The applications were siloed.

00:08:41.890 --> 00:08:43.990
But what that means
it made it difficult

00:08:43.990 --> 00:08:47.650
for us to provide a common
customer experience there.

00:08:47.650 --> 00:08:49.930
Those applications
were monolithic.

00:08:49.930 --> 00:08:53.680
Some of them are getting
long in the tooth.

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:57.460
Some of them have been in
place for 20 years or so.

00:08:57.460 --> 00:09:00.730
They were implemented
back in the days when

00:09:00.730 --> 00:09:05.224
we did monolithic systems
and tightly coupled and all

00:09:05.224 --> 00:09:06.140
of that kind of thing.

00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:09.070
So ultimately that just
makes those applications

00:09:09.070 --> 00:09:12.530
costly and
challenging to change.

00:09:12.530 --> 00:09:15.910
A small change in one
of these applications

00:09:15.910 --> 00:09:19.120
requires that we go through
and do all kinds of regression

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:21.640
testing to make
sure that we haven't

00:09:21.640 --> 00:09:24.360
broken things unintentionally.

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:27.340
And that's manually intensive.

00:09:27.340 --> 00:09:29.260
All of these applications
are delivered

00:09:29.260 --> 00:09:32.170
through on-premise
infrastructure.

00:09:32.170 --> 00:09:38.620
And not that that's a bad thing,
but in some ways it's limiting.

00:09:38.620 --> 00:09:42.640
We don't have a lot of
flexibility or ability

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:47.320
to deliver new infrastructure
and capabilities rapidly.

00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:51.110
And then, you know, with a
seasonal business like ours,

00:09:51.110 --> 00:09:54.490
as you might imagine, during the
holidays, we get really busy.

00:09:54.490 --> 00:09:59.350
A lot of the rest of the
year, we're much less busy.

00:09:59.350 --> 00:10:02.590
But we have to plan for
that capacity all year long.

00:10:06.700 --> 00:10:12.190
So we took a step back and tried
to come up with or understand

00:10:12.190 --> 00:10:15.220
what we needed to
change in order

00:10:15.220 --> 00:10:19.680
to solve some of these issues.

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:22.620
We came up with what we
described, what we called,

00:10:22.620 --> 00:10:25.200
our omnichannel architecture.

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:28.920
And this architecture
really was designed

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:32.970
to meet the goals of supporting
these consistent customer

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:34.720
experiences.

00:10:34.720 --> 00:10:39.060
We also needed it to improve
our agility and our speed

00:10:39.060 --> 00:10:41.670
to market, getting new
capabilities out there.

00:10:41.670 --> 00:10:45.600
And we needed it to be built
on a modern architecture.

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:48.990
I've been around this
merry-go-round enough times

00:10:48.990 --> 00:10:51.390
that this will probably
be about the third time

00:10:51.390 --> 00:10:54.150
that we've re-imagined
and re-envisioned

00:10:54.150 --> 00:10:56.920
these applications to
serve our customers.

00:10:56.920 --> 00:11:02.410
And so we're just sort of
planning for that next cycle

00:11:02.410 --> 00:11:07.930
to make sure it can
live that life cycle.

00:11:11.910 --> 00:11:14.010
So the vision that we created--

00:11:16.910 --> 00:11:18.620
what we found that
we needed to do

00:11:18.620 --> 00:11:23.420
is we had sort of envisioned a
lot of different capabilities

00:11:23.420 --> 00:11:28.130
around microservices
and cloud platforms

00:11:28.130 --> 00:11:29.850
and all of this kind of thing.

00:11:29.850 --> 00:11:33.410
But when you want to sell
that to an executive,

00:11:33.410 --> 00:11:35.460
you got to simplify it.

00:11:35.460 --> 00:11:40.400
And so what we did is created
sort of this one-page vision

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:47.250
document that described what
it is that we wanted to do.

00:11:47.250 --> 00:11:50.000
And I'm not going to go into
a lot of details on this.

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:57.260
But the idea here was that
this isn't the system design.

00:11:57.260 --> 00:11:59.410
This is really a tool
just for communicating

00:11:59.410 --> 00:12:01.780
with those executives.

00:12:01.780 --> 00:12:07.720
And what we found is that
having something like this

00:12:07.720 --> 00:12:11.140
was really important,
because every time we went

00:12:11.140 --> 00:12:14.860
into a meeting with those
executives, you know,

00:12:14.860 --> 00:12:17.890
we they're busy.

00:12:17.890 --> 00:12:19.480
They got a lot on their minds.

00:12:19.480 --> 00:12:21.340
And we had to kind
of refresh them

00:12:21.340 --> 00:12:23.220
on what it is we're
talking about,

00:12:23.220 --> 00:12:25.605
why are we talking about
it, and that kind of thing.

00:12:25.605 --> 00:12:26.980
And so having
something like this

00:12:26.980 --> 00:12:29.740
was really valuable
for doing them.

00:12:29.740 --> 00:12:36.850
It was also eye opening in
that, obviously, executives

00:12:36.850 --> 00:12:38.550
sort of know what
we do as a business.

00:12:38.550 --> 00:12:41.050
But when you start to put all
of the pieces kind of in front

00:12:41.050 --> 00:12:44.110
of them, then it starts
to open their eyes

00:12:44.110 --> 00:12:49.030
up a little bit about just all
of the complexity that's there.

00:12:49.030 --> 00:12:50.570
When you do something
like this, one

00:12:50.570 --> 00:12:52.570
of the things you want
to do is use a little bit

00:12:52.570 --> 00:12:54.790
of artistic license.

00:12:54.790 --> 00:12:59.610
And so for example, just
as you look at the picture,

00:12:59.610 --> 00:13:02.540
you can see that the systems
on the bottom, the enterprise

00:13:02.540 --> 00:13:05.420
applications, don't
really compare

00:13:05.420 --> 00:13:07.790
in scale to what's on the top.

00:13:07.790 --> 00:13:11.600
Those are obviously
large, important critical

00:13:11.600 --> 00:13:14.420
applications, but
they're not the focus

00:13:14.420 --> 00:13:16.160
of what we're targeting.

00:13:16.160 --> 00:13:20.750
So they're just kind of a
little less in scale there.

00:13:23.270 --> 00:13:28.380
So anyway, the point of
this is create a vision.

00:13:28.380 --> 00:13:29.670
Make it simple.

00:13:29.670 --> 00:13:31.620
Put it on one page.

00:13:31.620 --> 00:13:33.930
What we found after
doing this as well

00:13:33.930 --> 00:13:38.070
is that other teams
started using this document

00:13:38.070 --> 00:13:43.020
in their presentations and
in their selling of what

00:13:43.020 --> 00:13:45.000
they wanted to do.

00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:49.860
So they could sort of
latch onto the success

00:13:49.860 --> 00:13:52.215
that we were having
here with our momentum.

00:13:58.720 --> 00:14:02.970
So I just kind of boiled down
that document a little bit

00:14:02.970 --> 00:14:05.370
so that we could talk
a little bit more

00:14:05.370 --> 00:14:11.280
about how Google Cloud
fits into all of this.

00:14:11.280 --> 00:14:15.090
So at the top of the
diagram, we had our customer.

00:14:15.090 --> 00:14:18.360
We have our customer
interaction applications.

00:14:18.360 --> 00:14:22.880
And that could be our
point of sale system

00:14:22.880 --> 00:14:27.770
or a customer-facing website
or mobile web, custom store

00:14:27.770 --> 00:14:29.490
applications,
contact center, etc.

00:14:32.030 --> 00:14:34.850
All of those applications
in our vision

00:14:34.850 --> 00:14:38.000
and what we're working toward
is that those applications

00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:41.330
share capabilities through
shared services, which

00:14:41.330 --> 00:14:43.970
are exposed as APIs.

00:14:43.970 --> 00:14:48.020
And those shared services
could either be custom built.

00:14:48.020 --> 00:14:50.390
They could be purchased.

00:14:50.390 --> 00:14:54.540
Or they could be legacy, like
in the case of our commerce

00:14:54.540 --> 00:14:55.040
system.

00:14:58.070 --> 00:15:01.330
To date, we've
actually been working

00:15:01.330 --> 00:15:04.660
on several parts of this.

00:15:04.660 --> 00:15:09.790
We have several pieces
in production today.

00:15:09.790 --> 00:15:12.760
So as an example, our
customer-facing website,

00:15:12.760 --> 00:15:16.180
the UI layer of our
customer facing website,

00:15:16.180 --> 00:15:19.510
many of the pages that you see
if you go to our website today

00:15:19.510 --> 00:15:24.950
are actually delivered
with this architecture.

00:15:24.950 --> 00:15:28.630
Some core services, like
our purchase history,

00:15:28.630 --> 00:15:30.730
inventory availability
service, are delivered

00:15:30.730 --> 00:15:33.550
as custom services today.

00:15:33.550 --> 00:15:37.780
And we are in the process
of implementing a promotion

00:15:37.780 --> 00:15:42.010
service that we actually
purchased as we speak.

00:15:44.650 --> 00:15:47.260
All of this is tied together
through an API gateway.

00:15:47.260 --> 00:15:51.910
We are using Apigee as
our API gateway today.

00:15:51.910 --> 00:15:55.150
We've actually been using
Apigee for probably three

00:15:55.150 --> 00:15:56.470
plus years at this point.

00:15:56.470 --> 00:16:00.220
So we actually had a pretty good
base of APIs already in Apigee.

00:16:03.740 --> 00:16:08.100
And then just to sort of
where does Google Cloud fit

00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:09.680
into this?

00:16:09.680 --> 00:16:11.910
That customer facing
UI, like you mentioned,

00:16:11.910 --> 00:16:17.130
runs on GCP and GKE.

00:16:17.130 --> 00:16:20.870
Those custom services
also run in GKE.

00:16:20.870 --> 00:16:23.520
And that purchase
service will run in GKE.

00:16:23.520 --> 00:16:29.285
We've got some on-prem
services that are not in GKE.

00:16:29.285 --> 00:16:33.270
They're really part of our
legacy commerce application.

00:16:33.270 --> 00:16:34.800
But we are leveraging those.

00:16:34.800 --> 00:16:38.020
And we've layered an API
layer on top of that.

00:16:43.790 --> 00:16:46.640
CHRIS CUMMINGS: So you've seen
where we started at LL Bean

00:16:46.640 --> 00:16:49.480
and you've seen where
we're trying to go.

00:16:49.480 --> 00:16:52.500
How we're trying to realize
this vision, what it looks like.

00:16:52.500 --> 00:16:55.314
So how did we come to use
Google Cloud Platform?

00:16:55.314 --> 00:16:56.230
Let's talk about that.

00:16:56.230 --> 00:16:57.860
It's a Google conference.

00:16:57.860 --> 00:17:02.270
And we chose GCP about
a year and a half ago.

00:17:02.270 --> 00:17:04.640
We didn't really
start there, though.

00:17:04.640 --> 00:17:08.240
The first thing we embraced
pretty strongly on this journey

00:17:08.240 --> 00:17:11.510
to realize the vision
was Kubernetes and really

00:17:11.510 --> 00:17:13.520
first and foremost containers.

00:17:13.520 --> 00:17:15.170
But then as we
looked for a place

00:17:15.170 --> 00:17:17.650
to operate those containers,
deploy, run them,

00:17:17.650 --> 00:17:19.609
we embraced Kubernetes.

00:17:19.609 --> 00:17:21.980
And we went after
the containers early

00:17:21.980 --> 00:17:25.280
because we just saw the
benefits, the immutability

00:17:25.280 --> 00:17:27.859
of those environments, the
ability to spin up environments

00:17:27.859 --> 00:17:31.070
rapidly during the dev
cycle, scale horizontally

00:17:31.070 --> 00:17:33.680
very easily if the
app's designed for it.

00:17:33.680 --> 00:17:35.410
It was just very compelling.

00:17:35.410 --> 00:17:38.270
So we embraced
containers, Kubernetes.

00:17:38.270 --> 00:17:41.180
That led us a bit naturally
to Google Cloud Platform.

00:17:41.180 --> 00:17:43.400
At that time there was
really not another game

00:17:43.400 --> 00:17:45.739
in town from the big providers.

00:17:45.739 --> 00:17:47.530
They were starting to
talk about Kubernetes

00:17:47.530 --> 00:17:50.630
and they weren't embracing it.

00:17:50.630 --> 00:17:52.190
That has changed in a big way.

00:17:52.190 --> 00:17:54.490
Everybody is
scrambling to catch up.

00:17:54.490 --> 00:17:57.450
But Google, still pretty
much the leader here.

00:17:57.450 --> 00:17:59.210
But that wasn't the only thing.

00:17:59.210 --> 00:18:02.180
Kubernetes is a great example
of Google's open source

00:18:02.180 --> 00:18:02.720
commitment.

00:18:02.720 --> 00:18:05.600
And we're benefiting
from that with a number

00:18:05.600 --> 00:18:08.300
of other applications
that we use.

00:18:08.300 --> 00:18:11.330
And we just really liked
that open source commitment.

00:18:11.330 --> 00:18:12.800
We like their whole
approach to it.

00:18:12.800 --> 00:18:14.810
We didn't see quite
the same stance

00:18:14.810 --> 00:18:17.000
with the other providers.

00:18:17.000 --> 00:18:18.470
Security architecture.

00:18:18.470 --> 00:18:21.470
There were some things about
Google's security architecture

00:18:21.470 --> 00:18:24.020
that we thought were very
appropriate for a cloud native

00:18:24.020 --> 00:18:24.560
environment.

00:18:24.560 --> 00:18:27.830
That zero trust
approach was different

00:18:27.830 --> 00:18:32.180
than traditional on-premise
approaches that were being used

00:18:32.180 --> 00:18:33.950
at the time we encountered it.

00:18:33.950 --> 00:18:36.110
But we could see the power.

00:18:36.110 --> 00:18:37.200
We embraced it.

00:18:37.200 --> 00:18:40.340
And we've kind of blended what
Google brings to the table

00:18:40.340 --> 00:18:44.270
there along with some security
capabilities that are there

00:18:44.270 --> 00:18:46.250
with container,
containerized apps.

00:18:46.250 --> 00:18:49.160
There are some things you can do
that are kind of new and unique

00:18:49.160 --> 00:18:50.520
in some ways.

00:18:50.520 --> 00:18:53.360
And then we've blended a
few on-premise measures

00:18:53.360 --> 00:18:55.670
into the mix for a pretty
healthy blend on the security

00:18:55.670 --> 00:18:56.990
front.

00:18:56.990 --> 00:18:58.880
A few other things
to hit quickly here--

00:18:58.880 --> 00:18:59.900
dynamic scale.

00:18:59.900 --> 00:19:01.310
Randy mentioned the seasonality.

00:19:01.310 --> 00:19:05.300
That's a big win for a
retailer, not surprising.

00:19:05.300 --> 00:19:09.380
Cloud native brings the ability
to position teams to innovate.

00:19:09.380 --> 00:19:11.930
A lot of that's by the
speed at which they

00:19:11.930 --> 00:19:14.270
can move to market,
the speed of which they

00:19:14.270 --> 00:19:15.890
can try new technologies.

00:19:15.890 --> 00:19:19.790
We really felt like we had the
tools necessary with Google

00:19:19.790 --> 00:19:21.210
in that area.

00:19:21.210 --> 00:19:23.182
And then finally,
being a retailer

00:19:23.182 --> 00:19:25.640
with digital marketing, there's
a key business relationship

00:19:25.640 --> 00:19:29.120
with LL Bean and Google that
we felt was an advantage.

00:19:29.120 --> 00:19:31.220
With Cloud native in
general, with some things

00:19:31.220 --> 00:19:34.970
with Google in particular,
we like the pricing model.

00:19:34.970 --> 00:19:39.740
So what does our deployment
look like on GCP?

00:19:39.740 --> 00:19:43.430
Well, I'm going to go yet more
simple here and just highlight

00:19:43.430 --> 00:19:45.890
three major components
that make up

00:19:45.890 --> 00:19:48.380
the core of what we are doing.

00:19:48.380 --> 00:19:53.880
Google Cloud native, GCP, GKE
is obviously a key part of that.

00:19:53.880 --> 00:19:56.330
But over the top
of that, we've laid

00:19:56.330 --> 00:19:58.850
a microservices
architecture down.

00:19:58.850 --> 00:20:01.610
And we really
embraced microservices

00:20:01.610 --> 00:20:03.170
earliest in the game here.

00:20:03.170 --> 00:20:06.110
And we really embraced
the principles

00:20:06.110 --> 00:20:09.650
behind microservices, the
domain-driven design concepts

00:20:09.650 --> 00:20:13.430
that are there, the
separation of concerns,

00:20:13.430 --> 00:20:15.950
the loosely coupled
applications,

00:20:15.950 --> 00:20:21.350
bounded context, language having
meaning within those contexts.

00:20:21.350 --> 00:20:24.320
And we also embrace
the idea that teams

00:20:24.320 --> 00:20:28.910
could align with application
domains, business domains.

00:20:28.910 --> 00:20:31.700
And when you mix all that into
a microservices environment

00:20:31.700 --> 00:20:34.070
deployed the right
way, it really

00:20:34.070 --> 00:20:37.220
allows you to move
all the pieces forward

00:20:37.220 --> 00:20:38.750
at their own pace.

00:20:38.750 --> 00:20:42.260
It really, really changed
the game for LL Bean.

00:20:42.260 --> 00:20:44.120
At the time,
microservices weren't

00:20:44.120 --> 00:20:47.150
as widely adopted outside of
the big internet companies.

00:20:47.150 --> 00:20:49.670
It's become much more
industry standard now

00:20:49.670 --> 00:20:51.460
and much more accepted.

00:20:51.460 --> 00:20:56.060
Also, here, dev ops pipeline
is a key part of the puzzle.

00:20:56.060 --> 00:20:58.670
Continuous integration
and delivery,

00:20:58.670 --> 00:21:01.140
that's how everything
gets built,

00:21:01.140 --> 00:21:03.580
tested, stress tested,
vulnerabilities

00:21:03.580 --> 00:21:06.770
scanned, deployed, monitored,
maintained, all of that.

00:21:06.770 --> 00:21:08.930
There is a strong
pipeline here that

00:21:08.930 --> 00:21:11.270
is a key part of
everything that goes out

00:21:11.270 --> 00:21:13.220
onto the platform and
a key part of what

00:21:13.220 --> 00:21:15.540
we consider our platform.

00:21:15.540 --> 00:21:17.120
So this looks really simple.

00:21:17.120 --> 00:21:18.320
Is it really that simple?

00:21:18.320 --> 00:21:20.370
No, it's really not that simple.

00:21:20.370 --> 00:21:22.790
There's actually a
lot more going on

00:21:22.790 --> 00:21:24.590
than that simple picture shows.

00:21:24.590 --> 00:21:27.920
There is a healthy mix
of things that Google

00:21:27.920 --> 00:21:30.050
provides as a service.

00:21:30.050 --> 00:21:31.640
But there's also
a bunch of stuff

00:21:31.640 --> 00:21:32.990
that we've brought to the table.

00:21:32.990 --> 00:21:34.880
And really, the
point of this slide

00:21:34.880 --> 00:21:37.050
is not to talk about
each one of these things,

00:21:37.050 --> 00:21:39.710
but to highlight that,
the Cloud native approach

00:21:39.710 --> 00:21:43.410
we chose allows us a lot of
flexibility to mix the bits

00:21:43.410 --> 00:21:47.580
and pieces together that we
feel are right for a given job.

00:21:47.580 --> 00:21:53.940
And examples are GCP, the
backbone GCP itself, Compute

00:21:53.940 --> 00:21:57.210
Engine, Kubernetes Engine,
things like Pub/Sub for event

00:21:57.210 --> 00:22:00.450
messaging, Cloud
Spanner as a service,

00:22:00.450 --> 00:22:03.300
relational database that we
use for inventory availability

00:22:03.300 --> 00:22:04.620
and some other things.

00:22:04.620 --> 00:22:07.450
All great stuff we get
as managed services.

00:22:07.450 --> 00:22:10.080
But on the stuff
we've rolled, we

00:22:10.080 --> 00:22:13.500
have key dev components,
dev pipeline stuff,

00:22:13.500 --> 00:22:17.670
GitHub, Jenkins, CI,
Nexus Artifact Repository.

00:22:17.670 --> 00:22:20.250
We use Terraform for
infrastructure automation.

00:22:20.250 --> 00:22:23.550
And then just as an
example, MongoDB,

00:22:23.550 --> 00:22:26.970
we chose just to go with MongoDB
for some reasons that were

00:22:26.970 --> 00:22:29.020
very specific to our use case.

00:22:29.020 --> 00:22:33.130
But we were able to deploy that
as a service for our dev teams.

00:22:33.130 --> 00:22:36.360
So from a dev team standpoint,
they need a relational DB.

00:22:36.360 --> 00:22:40.410
They've got an option with
Spanner and with other things.

00:22:40.410 --> 00:22:42.810
But with a document
store DB, they've

00:22:42.810 --> 00:22:45.000
got an option with Mongo,
and they don't have

00:22:45.000 --> 00:22:46.374
to go build that themselves.

00:22:46.374 --> 00:22:47.040
They can use it.

00:22:47.040 --> 00:22:50.340
So powerful mix going
with Cloud native

00:22:50.340 --> 00:22:54.180
and putting the pieces out
there to enable the dev teams.

00:22:54.180 --> 00:22:57.150
But how do we do
all that at LL Bean?

00:22:57.150 --> 00:22:59.760
Well, one of the things that
we did early in the process

00:22:59.760 --> 00:23:04.180
is we established what we
call a platform services team.

00:23:04.180 --> 00:23:05.580
And this team has a mission.

00:23:05.580 --> 00:23:08.580
The mission is to maintain
the systems and processes

00:23:08.580 --> 00:23:12.420
necessary for agile and
continuous software delivery

00:23:12.420 --> 00:23:15.480
and furthermore to enable
the dev teams to embrace

00:23:15.480 --> 00:23:17.530
DevOps culture and practice.

00:23:17.530 --> 00:23:20.320
So these guys aren't
the development team.

00:23:20.320 --> 00:23:24.720
These guys are the platform
team who maintains the platform,

00:23:24.720 --> 00:23:28.140
stewards it forward, takes
care of security concerns,

00:23:28.140 --> 00:23:31.410
manages and maintains
that DevOps pipeline,

00:23:31.410 --> 00:23:32.850
and a myriad of other things.

00:23:32.850 --> 00:23:35.040
They collaborate heavily
with the dev teams.

00:23:35.040 --> 00:23:38.010
Our dev teams build software
applications using all of this,

00:23:38.010 --> 00:23:40.070
and they deploy them.

00:23:40.070 --> 00:23:42.540
Just a few practice
areas to highlight.

00:23:42.540 --> 00:23:44.850
And their mantra--
and those of you

00:23:44.850 --> 00:23:47.910
who have read "The Continuous
Delivery Book" and "The DevOps

00:23:47.910 --> 00:23:50.580
Handbook," you see some things
that probably look familiar

00:23:50.580 --> 00:23:51.210
here.

00:23:51.210 --> 00:23:52.730
But automate
everything, if you're

00:23:52.730 --> 00:23:54.390
doing microservices of scale.

00:23:54.390 --> 00:23:56.337
If you're going cloud
native, you can't really

00:23:56.337 --> 00:23:58.920
have manual steps in all this
if you're going to be efficient.

00:23:58.920 --> 00:24:00.990
It just doesn't work.

00:24:00.990 --> 00:24:04.442
But they do things like
infrastructure automation,

00:24:04.442 --> 00:24:06.900
scripting this stuff to create
the systems and configs that

00:24:06.900 --> 00:24:09.510
are necessary, letting
teams spin environments

00:24:09.510 --> 00:24:11.580
really quickly and easily.

00:24:11.580 --> 00:24:14.760
Everything to do with continuous
delivery is in their domain.

00:24:14.760 --> 00:24:17.160
And then site reliability
engineering, you know,

00:24:17.160 --> 00:24:18.700
we want to push out fast.

00:24:18.700 --> 00:24:21.840
We also wanted to be stable,
get immediate feedback,

00:24:21.840 --> 00:24:24.540
react to that and
make sure we've

00:24:24.540 --> 00:24:26.670
got healthy systems go forward.

00:24:26.670 --> 00:24:29.730
So a big lesson learned--
we'll share a few more lessons

00:24:29.730 --> 00:24:31.330
learned here in a few slides--

00:24:31.330 --> 00:24:34.290
but strongly recommend
you consider,

00:24:34.290 --> 00:24:37.434
if you've got some magnitude
in your environment, a platform

00:24:37.434 --> 00:24:38.850
services team to
help you forward.

00:24:44.035 --> 00:24:45.660
RANDY DYER: Great,
so before I move on,

00:24:45.660 --> 00:24:48.300
one thing I wanted to add to
what Chris was just saying

00:24:48.300 --> 00:24:51.510
is on that list, which I won't
flip back to, but on that list

00:24:51.510 --> 00:24:55.140
you might have seen Cloud
Spanner on there for example.

00:24:55.140 --> 00:24:58.010
And Cloud Spanner
in some ways might

00:24:58.010 --> 00:25:01.111
be a bit overkill for what
we're using it for today.

00:25:01.111 --> 00:25:02.610
We've got some other
use cases where

00:25:02.610 --> 00:25:03.693
it will be more effective.

00:25:03.693 --> 00:25:07.320
But one of the
things about that is

00:25:07.320 --> 00:25:12.260
that I want my teams to be
focused on the customer,

00:25:12.260 --> 00:25:13.950
delivering customer value.

00:25:13.950 --> 00:25:17.220
I don't want my team to have
to become technology experts

00:25:17.220 --> 00:25:19.320
and learn how to do
things like stand up

00:25:19.320 --> 00:25:22.440
database environments
that are highly available

00:25:22.440 --> 00:25:24.400
and all of that kind of stuff.

00:25:24.400 --> 00:25:26.310
There are technology
providers that

00:25:26.310 --> 00:25:29.770
can do that much
better than we can

00:25:29.770 --> 00:25:31.360
and much more
efficiently than we can.

00:25:31.360 --> 00:25:33.609
And that's why we want to
leverage that kind of thing.

00:25:33.609 --> 00:25:35.190
So I just wanted
to call that out.

00:25:37.912 --> 00:25:39.620
One of the things I'm
going to talk about

00:25:39.620 --> 00:25:42.357
is one of the examples
of applications

00:25:42.357 --> 00:25:43.940
that we've delivered,
and I've already

00:25:43.940 --> 00:25:46.940
mentioned it, that's the LL
Bean website, or at least the UI

00:25:46.940 --> 00:25:49.550
layer of that
llbean.com website.

00:25:49.550 --> 00:25:52.370
And the reason I want to
talk about is because--

00:25:52.370 --> 00:26:00.200
I'm going to talk a little bit
about how this platform has

00:26:00.200 --> 00:26:04.100
really given us some
capabilities that we

00:26:04.100 --> 00:26:07.160
didn't have before there.

00:26:07.160 --> 00:26:10.740
So in order to
really explain this,

00:26:10.740 --> 00:26:16.830
I got to talk a little bit
about how our legacy website was

00:26:16.830 --> 00:26:19.360
built and architected.

00:26:19.360 --> 00:26:22.530
We first implemented it back
in 1996, like I mentioned.

00:26:22.530 --> 00:26:26.610
It underwent a big
technology change in 2001

00:26:26.610 --> 00:26:31.660
when we switched it over to the
WebSphere Commerce platform,

00:26:31.660 --> 00:26:35.490
all based on Java
and JEE and all

00:26:35.490 --> 00:26:38.220
of those kinds of technologies.

00:26:38.220 --> 00:26:40.440
The UI layer at
the time was really

00:26:40.440 --> 00:26:45.690
part of that monolith,
implemented in JSPs.

00:26:45.690 --> 00:26:52.790
And as time goes by, the
site became more dynamic.

00:26:52.790 --> 00:26:55.120
We started layering
on lots of JavaScript

00:26:55.120 --> 00:27:00.690
to make everything
dynamic and robust.

00:27:00.690 --> 00:27:05.700
And then as new devices
started to become prominent,

00:27:05.700 --> 00:27:09.760
then we added more JavaScript
to handle those kinds of things.

00:27:09.760 --> 00:27:16.440
So anyway, it got to the point
where that UI application was

00:27:16.440 --> 00:27:20.610
just very complex and fragile
and difficult to maintain

00:27:20.610 --> 00:27:21.880
and all of that.

00:27:21.880 --> 00:27:23.385
So that was another thing.

00:27:23.385 --> 00:27:25.260
So we said we got to
step back and figure out

00:27:25.260 --> 00:27:27.250
a better way to do this.

00:27:27.250 --> 00:27:30.420
So we came up with what
we called just simply

00:27:30.420 --> 00:27:32.600
the front-end architecture.

00:27:32.600 --> 00:27:35.910
And what front-end
architecture is

00:27:35.910 --> 00:27:41.340
is a framework that
we put together,

00:27:41.340 --> 00:27:48.000
based primarily on React and
other open source libraries,

00:27:48.000 --> 00:27:52.860
all based in node JS.

00:27:52.860 --> 00:27:56.670
And this architecture allows
us to deliver customer facing

00:27:56.670 --> 00:28:00.900
applications that will run
on either desktop, mobile,

00:28:00.900 --> 00:28:05.490
or tablets, any
form factors there.

00:28:05.490 --> 00:28:09.170
And like I mentioned, we are
using it to replace that UI

00:28:09.170 --> 00:28:11.790
layer on llbean.com today.

00:28:11.790 --> 00:28:14.100
And we have plans to use
it for other capabilities

00:28:14.100 --> 00:28:15.540
as well, like in
our call centers.

00:28:18.900 --> 00:28:23.760
Not only is it using React,
but the architecture also

00:28:23.760 --> 00:28:25.830
defines how we use React.

00:28:25.830 --> 00:28:31.440
So it employs an
atomic design model

00:28:31.440 --> 00:28:36.180
that really mandates that
all the other components are

00:28:36.180 --> 00:28:40.410
loosely coupled
and independently

00:28:40.410 --> 00:28:43.860
developable and deployable.

00:28:43.860 --> 00:28:48.630
And obviously, it supports
responsive web design

00:28:48.630 --> 00:28:52.290
and accessibility
requirements out of the box.

00:28:52.290 --> 00:28:55.070
And we've found that
since we've implemented,

00:28:55.070 --> 00:28:59.880
it's actually been a pretty
good improvement in performance

00:28:59.880 --> 00:29:04.480
and gives us some scalability
and speed to market with this.

00:29:08.610 --> 00:29:11.340
And it just is a
platform that gives us

00:29:11.340 --> 00:29:18.300
the ability to support ongoing
experience optimization.

00:29:18.300 --> 00:29:22.730
So I mentioned that we
have gone to production

00:29:22.730 --> 00:29:24.830
with many of the
pages on our website

00:29:24.830 --> 00:29:27.320
under this architecture.

00:29:27.320 --> 00:29:30.920
And that deployment obviously
is on Google Cloud Platform

00:29:30.920 --> 00:29:34.580
or in Google Kubernetes engine.

00:29:37.370 --> 00:29:40.040
So what the value
of the platform

00:29:40.040 --> 00:29:41.810
brought to all of
this, though, is

00:29:41.810 --> 00:29:48.185
that, other than the scaling and
things I mentioned, is that--

00:29:48.185 --> 00:29:49.940
so a little case study here.

00:29:49.940 --> 00:29:52.910
A few weeks ago, we
implemented a new version

00:29:52.910 --> 00:29:55.790
of this application
that included capability

00:29:55.790 --> 00:29:59.090
around the category pages.

00:29:59.090 --> 00:30:01.880
And shortly after
we were deployed,

00:30:01.880 --> 00:30:06.650
we started getting some
alerts from the monitoring

00:30:06.650 --> 00:30:08.270
that we had set up.

00:30:08.270 --> 00:30:10.700
And those alerts were
telling us that Kubernetes

00:30:10.700 --> 00:30:14.410
was doing lots of pod restarts.

00:30:14.410 --> 00:30:16.750
And so when we
looked into it, it

00:30:16.750 --> 00:30:19.170
was doing the pod
restarts because we

00:30:19.170 --> 00:30:23.860
had seen that the
memory usage started

00:30:23.860 --> 00:30:26.170
climbing pretty rapidly.

00:30:26.170 --> 00:30:29.242
And Kubernetes, we'd set it
up with some thresholds that

00:30:29.242 --> 00:30:31.450
says, when you get to a
certain point in memory usage

00:30:31.450 --> 00:30:33.010
restart itself so
that everything

00:30:33.010 --> 00:30:36.320
stays clean and running.

00:30:36.320 --> 00:30:38.860
And so that's what it was doing.

00:30:41.470 --> 00:30:46.520
So because it was doing
that, customers never

00:30:46.520 --> 00:30:51.920
saw that we were having
an issue and never

00:30:51.920 --> 00:30:55.250
had any downtime or
anything like that.

00:30:55.250 --> 00:30:59.929
I point this out because if this
were our legacy website that

00:30:59.929 --> 00:31:01.970
doesn't have these
capabilities-- it doesn't have

00:31:01.970 --> 00:31:05.950
something like Kubernetes
doing this kind of management--

00:31:05.950 --> 00:31:08.120
then it probably
would have keeled over

00:31:08.120 --> 00:31:11.630
and twitched its
feet for a while.

00:31:11.630 --> 00:31:16.080
But that didn't
happen in this case.

00:31:16.080 --> 00:31:18.260
The other part about this,
like Chris mentioned,

00:31:18.260 --> 00:31:21.830
we've got the CI/CD
pipeline in place

00:31:21.830 --> 00:31:25.190
that allows us to
do rapid deployments

00:31:25.190 --> 00:31:27.620
and frequent deployments.

00:31:27.620 --> 00:31:31.100
So as part of the problem
resolution to all of this,

00:31:31.100 --> 00:31:35.840
we were able to get in,
analyze what was going on,

00:31:35.840 --> 00:31:40.325
make changes, and get it
into production quickly.

00:31:40.325 --> 00:31:42.800
It turned out that the problem
was actually a little deeper

00:31:42.800 --> 00:31:44.520
than what we thought.

00:31:44.520 --> 00:31:51.380
So the first implementation
didn't solve the problem.

00:31:51.380 --> 00:31:57.140
But because, again, the rapid
ability to turn things around,

00:31:57.140 --> 00:32:01.270
we were able to get the issue
solved in about four hours.

00:32:01.270 --> 00:32:03.170
On our legacy
site, that probably

00:32:03.170 --> 00:32:08.600
would have been at least
a day and an overnight

00:32:08.600 --> 00:32:13.550
in order to solve something like
that and likely, very likely,

00:32:13.550 --> 00:32:16.430
would have been an
outage to the website.

00:32:16.430 --> 00:32:20.870
So that's just an example
of some of the advantages

00:32:20.870 --> 00:32:22.820
that we've seen on
the platform so far.

00:32:26.919 --> 00:32:28.960
CHRIS CUMMINGS: OK, so I
mentioned some learnings

00:32:28.960 --> 00:32:30.050
we were going to share.

00:32:30.050 --> 00:32:35.150
And we picked five things to
raise up and highlight here.

00:32:35.150 --> 00:32:38.350
In reality, we are
practicing agile here.

00:32:38.350 --> 00:32:42.550
So we are doing iterations,
retros, continuously learning,

00:32:42.550 --> 00:32:43.870
and trying to improve.

00:32:43.870 --> 00:32:46.930
But when you step back and you
look at the big picture of what

00:32:46.930 --> 00:32:48.400
we were doing, these stood out.

00:32:48.400 --> 00:32:51.304
And we thought they would
be worth sharing with you.

00:32:51.304 --> 00:32:53.470
Couple of them you've already
heard something about.

00:32:53.470 --> 00:32:56.650
Number one, establish that
vision for the future.

00:32:56.650 --> 00:32:58.990
You saw what the one
for LL Bean looked like.

00:32:58.990 --> 00:33:00.340
Yours will look different.

00:33:00.340 --> 00:33:03.800
But it's really important
that you do that upfront

00:33:03.800 --> 00:33:06.820
and you do it in a way that
everyone in the organization

00:33:06.820 --> 00:33:09.080
who needs to can embrace it.

00:33:09.080 --> 00:33:11.740
So the IT on the ground
folks, the developers

00:33:11.740 --> 00:33:15.040
have to be able to understand
that the IT management has

00:33:15.040 --> 00:33:15.940
to get on board.

00:33:15.940 --> 00:33:19.750
But those executives
have to understand

00:33:19.750 --> 00:33:24.140
at the right level what this
is that we're trying to do.

00:33:24.140 --> 00:33:25.570
And the reason
that's important is

00:33:25.570 --> 00:33:27.760
because this is not a project.

00:33:27.760 --> 00:33:29.240
This is a journey.

00:33:29.240 --> 00:33:32.590
So if you have an
organization of any magnitude

00:33:32.590 --> 00:33:34.450
that you're trying
to do this in,

00:33:34.450 --> 00:33:36.700
it's going to be a
multi-year commitment.

00:33:36.700 --> 00:33:38.470
And that multi-year
commitment is

00:33:38.470 --> 00:33:40.680
going to have to have
executive sponsorship.

00:33:40.680 --> 00:33:42.530
It's going to have
to have budget.

00:33:42.530 --> 00:33:45.040
There's no way you can
get there without selling

00:33:45.040 --> 00:33:47.830
that this is a
journey worth taking

00:33:47.830 --> 00:33:51.880
and that vision is
critical to do that.

00:33:51.880 --> 00:33:55.090
It's OK to start with
prototype projects

00:33:55.090 --> 00:33:56.320
and such at the outset.

00:33:56.320 --> 00:33:57.130
We did that.

00:33:57.130 --> 00:33:59.980
But really, you can't think
about it as a project.

00:33:59.980 --> 00:34:00.700
Don't do that.

00:34:00.700 --> 00:34:01.750
But do iterate.

00:34:01.750 --> 00:34:06.580
You can do this by iterating,
by bringing business value

00:34:06.580 --> 00:34:10.719
along the way, by learning
and changing course as you go.

00:34:10.719 --> 00:34:12.639
But it's going to take time.

00:34:12.639 --> 00:34:15.850
Third one, embrace DevOps
is what we summed it up as.

00:34:15.850 --> 00:34:19.210
But what it really means
is to get the ROI out

00:34:19.210 --> 00:34:22.210
of a big modernization
effort, you

00:34:22.210 --> 00:34:24.790
have to recognize it's more
than just the technology.

00:34:24.790 --> 00:34:26.780
It's the people and the process.

00:34:26.780 --> 00:34:29.620
You won't get the
benefit if you keep

00:34:29.620 --> 00:34:31.600
trying to build software
the way you've always

00:34:31.600 --> 00:34:34.989
built it, if you're not
willing to reorganize where

00:34:34.989 --> 00:34:38.199
appropriate, to align
teams with product domains,

00:34:38.199 --> 00:34:40.570
allow them some
independence, and evolving

00:34:40.570 --> 00:34:44.080
those and deploying them, let
them fail fast and recover.

00:34:44.080 --> 00:34:47.290
It's a different way of
thinking for a lot of companies.

00:34:47.290 --> 00:34:51.250
And it's frankly way harder
than the technology here.

00:34:51.250 --> 00:34:53.440
We kind of challenged
pretty much everything

00:34:53.440 --> 00:34:55.690
that LL Bean was doing
with software development

00:34:55.690 --> 00:34:57.610
when we went when
we went after this.

00:34:57.610 --> 00:35:00.886
But it's worth doing.

00:35:00.886 --> 00:35:02.510
Fourth thing I've
already talked about.

00:35:02.510 --> 00:35:04.764
Establish a platform
services team.

00:35:04.764 --> 00:35:06.430
Especially if you're
going cloud native,

00:35:06.430 --> 00:35:08.990
especially if you have more
than a few development teams,

00:35:08.990 --> 00:35:10.760
it will pay off.

00:35:10.760 --> 00:35:13.840
And then finally, engage
security at the outset.

00:35:13.840 --> 00:35:15.560
We hear this all the time.

00:35:15.560 --> 00:35:18.790
People are always telling us
get security on board up front.

00:35:18.790 --> 00:35:19.780
We did this.

00:35:19.780 --> 00:35:23.960
And in hindsight, if we had
waited six months, 12 months,

00:35:23.960 --> 00:35:28.210
18 months down the road and then
tried to retrofit security in,

00:35:28.210 --> 00:35:29.950
it would not have
been a good outcome.

00:35:29.950 --> 00:35:33.130
It would have been very
costly and painful.

00:35:33.130 --> 00:35:35.110
It would have disrupted
development tracks.

00:35:35.110 --> 00:35:37.630
And it's pretty
likely it would not

00:35:37.630 --> 00:35:39.820
have been as good an
outcome as we have now.

00:35:39.820 --> 00:35:41.620
But we did this upfront.

00:35:41.620 --> 00:35:44.300
And the way we did
that-- twofold.

00:35:44.300 --> 00:35:48.260
One, we established an executive
sponsorship team, a steering

00:35:48.260 --> 00:35:51.820
committee, if you
will, at LL Bean.

00:35:51.820 --> 00:35:54.700
Key business execs who had a
stake in what we were doing

00:35:54.700 --> 00:35:58.720
and the right IT management
resources, the head of security

00:35:58.720 --> 00:36:00.330
was on that committee.

00:36:00.330 --> 00:36:01.780
And so he was
there at the outset

00:36:01.780 --> 00:36:03.738
as we started talking
about going to the cloud,

00:36:03.738 --> 00:36:05.170
going cloud native.

00:36:05.170 --> 00:36:07.260
When we established
a platform team,

00:36:07.260 --> 00:36:10.720
we added a security
architect to that team.

00:36:10.720 --> 00:36:13.600
So that individual was
there right up front.

00:36:13.600 --> 00:36:14.800
And it's paid off.

00:36:14.800 --> 00:36:17.410
We really had no idea
where the security path was

00:36:17.410 --> 00:36:18.700
going to lead when we started.

00:36:18.700 --> 00:36:22.480
And we found our way through,
and we continue to evolve.

00:36:22.480 --> 00:36:24.310
But we found it to a good place.

00:36:24.310 --> 00:36:26.270
So just a few lessons there.

00:36:26.270 --> 00:36:29.080
There are probably hundreds
or thousands of micro lessons

00:36:29.080 --> 00:36:31.740
you come across as you
do something like this.

00:36:31.740 --> 00:36:34.180
Five that we thought were
worth mentioning to you.

00:36:38.090 --> 00:36:38.840
RANDY DYER: Great.

00:36:38.840 --> 00:36:41.550
So where are we going from here?

00:36:41.550 --> 00:36:46.510
So we mentioned we actually
started this effort

00:36:46.510 --> 00:36:48.250
a couple of years ago.

00:36:48.250 --> 00:36:52.710
At the time, GKE was
pretty early in the market.

00:36:52.710 --> 00:36:55.330
And I don't remember exactly
which version we started on,

00:36:55.330 --> 00:36:58.230
maybe 1.4 or
something like that.

00:36:58.230 --> 00:37:03.410
So we had to do a lot of
work to secure things,

00:37:03.410 --> 00:37:08.200
to get things interconnected
the way they needed to be

00:37:08.200 --> 00:37:10.690
with what we had at the time.

00:37:10.690 --> 00:37:13.240
Obviously, since
then, things like SDO

00:37:13.240 --> 00:37:15.850
are coming on board that can
solve a lot of the problems

00:37:15.850 --> 00:37:19.100
that we had to kind of
work around at the time.

00:37:19.100 --> 00:37:21.490
So part of this journey
is to continually

00:37:21.490 --> 00:37:24.760
evolve that platform
as new capabilities

00:37:24.760 --> 00:37:33.100
come in, as things
change, and to continue

00:37:33.100 --> 00:37:38.480
to build out that layer of
services that you saw earlier.

00:37:38.480 --> 00:37:40.450
So we've got active
development underway

00:37:40.450 --> 00:37:43.090
right now with some
new capabilities

00:37:43.090 --> 00:37:47.710
that we'll be rolling
out probably--

00:37:47.710 --> 00:37:49.270
we'll actually
complete development

00:37:49.270 --> 00:37:51.100
before our peak
season, but we actually

00:37:51.100 --> 00:37:53.662
won't roll them out until
after, just because we

00:37:53.662 --> 00:37:54.578
don't want to do that.

00:37:57.450 --> 00:38:00.530
And then the other
thing that we are doing

00:38:00.530 --> 00:38:02.900
is many of the legacy
systems that we're

00:38:02.900 --> 00:38:06.530
replacing at this point, like
I mentioned, are fairly old.

00:38:06.530 --> 00:38:09.990
Some of them are
very batch oriented.

00:38:09.990 --> 00:38:13.670
And so as we're going
through this re-architecting,

00:38:13.670 --> 00:38:15.500
we're really looking
at these from more

00:38:15.500 --> 00:38:18.790
of an event-based approach.

00:38:18.790 --> 00:38:20.960
And so part of the
work that we're doing

00:38:20.960 --> 00:38:26.230
is starting to layer some
event processing capabilities

00:38:26.230 --> 00:38:27.380
onto the platform.

00:38:31.050 --> 00:38:33.930
And then the other thing
that we've got going on

00:38:33.930 --> 00:38:38.160
is, as a direct marketer,
as you might expect,

00:38:38.160 --> 00:38:41.430
we've got a lot of data, a lot
of data about our customers

00:38:41.430 --> 00:38:43.080
and how they interact with us.

00:38:43.080 --> 00:38:48.900
And we use that data
to model for delivering

00:38:48.900 --> 00:38:52.810
catalogs and emails and all
of those kinds of things.

00:38:52.810 --> 00:38:58.200
So there's a lot of
analysis that we do.

00:38:58.200 --> 00:39:00.990
And up until this
point, we've done

00:39:00.990 --> 00:39:03.510
that really with your
traditional enterprise data

00:39:03.510 --> 00:39:06.860
warehouse capabilities
and tools like SAS

00:39:06.860 --> 00:39:09.480
and some of these other things.

00:39:09.480 --> 00:39:16.080
But again, as things
like the targeted ads

00:39:16.080 --> 00:39:17.550
and all of that
kind of thing just

00:39:17.550 --> 00:39:22.440
have created more and more
data that we need to process,

00:39:22.440 --> 00:39:26.640
and so our current
infrastructure is just running

00:39:26.640 --> 00:39:27.730
out of steam doing that.

00:39:27.730 --> 00:39:30.660
So we've got some
work underway where

00:39:30.660 --> 00:39:33.690
we are pulling a
lot of that data

00:39:33.690 --> 00:39:37.440
into the Google Cloud
Platform and using tools

00:39:37.440 --> 00:39:39.740
like BigQuery and Cloud--

00:39:42.990 --> 00:39:43.880
sorry-- Dataflow.

00:39:46.710 --> 00:39:50.610
With all these tools, I can't
keep track of all of them.

00:39:50.610 --> 00:39:52.490
So that work is underway.

00:39:55.780 --> 00:39:58.870
And like Chris
mentioned, we've got

00:39:58.870 --> 00:40:01.890
a roadmap for all of this
work that we've created.

00:40:01.890 --> 00:40:05.200
And we expect to be
continuing on this roadmap

00:40:05.200 --> 00:40:08.910
for at least the next few
years, as we bring all of this

00:40:08.910 --> 00:40:09.410
together.

00:40:14.940 --> 00:40:19.740
So we hope we've given you some
information and some insights

00:40:19.740 --> 00:40:22.410
into our journey.

00:40:22.410 --> 00:40:25.480
And hopefully they can help you.

00:40:25.480 --> 00:40:26.550
So thank you.

00:40:26.550 --> 00:40:30.210
And so before we leave, I
just want to invite all of you

00:40:30.210 --> 00:40:34.110
to also become an outsider,
enjoy the outdoors,

00:40:34.110 --> 00:40:42.000
and you can find us at
llbean.com up there.

00:40:42.000 --> 00:40:45.380
We are here for questions if
anybody wants to ask questions.

00:40:45.380 --> 00:40:47.250
We're also here
at the conference

00:40:47.250 --> 00:40:50.310
if anybody's interested in
chatting in more detail.

00:40:50.310 --> 00:40:52.160
We're certainly
willing to do that.

00:40:52.160 --> 00:40:53.802
So thank you.

00:40:53.802 --> 00:40:56.718
[APPLAUSE]

00:41:03.397 --> 00:41:05.480
CHRIS CUMMINGS: I guess
if anybody has a question,

00:41:05.480 --> 00:41:06.950
I actually don't
see a microphone.

00:41:06.950 --> 00:41:07.992
So you can just shout it.

00:41:07.992 --> 00:41:08.783
We're pretty close.

00:41:08.783 --> 00:41:09.390
Oh, I see it.

00:41:09.390 --> 00:41:10.264
There it is up there.

00:41:23.517 --> 00:41:24.350
AUDIENCE: Hey, guys.

00:41:24.350 --> 00:41:26.440
Thanks very much for
the presentation.

00:41:26.440 --> 00:41:30.100
Could I just ask you maybe a
little left field question?

00:41:30.100 --> 00:41:32.530
Did you ever consider
before starting this journey

00:41:32.530 --> 00:41:37.730
to use something commercial,
off the shelf, Commerce Cloud,

00:41:37.730 --> 00:41:40.270
Iberus, something like
this, before you went down

00:41:40.270 --> 00:41:45.420
this journey of creating
the developments?

00:41:45.420 --> 00:41:47.400
RANDY DYER: Yeah, in
fact, our current website

00:41:47.400 --> 00:41:50.831
is on a commercial
product, so to speak.

00:41:50.831 --> 00:41:51.955
It's on WebSphere Commerce.

00:41:55.440 --> 00:41:58.440
So we absolutely
did consider that.

00:41:58.440 --> 00:42:03.500
One of the things that we, in
our current implementation,

00:42:03.500 --> 00:42:08.730
our website is relatively
rich in capabilities.

00:42:08.730 --> 00:42:12.636
And we wanted to keep that.

00:42:12.636 --> 00:42:14.010
In our current
implementation, we

00:42:14.010 --> 00:42:18.640
had to do a lot of customization
in order to get there.

00:42:18.640 --> 00:42:21.090
And that was just
kind of a model

00:42:21.090 --> 00:42:30.170
that we didn't really want to
go down at this point in time.

00:42:30.170 --> 00:42:37.630
The other thing is, again, we've
got a pretty big presence still

00:42:37.630 --> 00:42:38.900
in the call centers.

00:42:38.900 --> 00:42:42.920
So we get a lot of orders
coming in over the phone.

00:42:42.920 --> 00:42:45.250
And so we really wanted
to be able to blend

00:42:45.250 --> 00:42:46.900
those channels together.

00:42:46.900 --> 00:42:48.970
And that was
something that we just

00:42:48.970 --> 00:42:54.560
didn't see really happening well
in sort of those products that

00:42:54.560 --> 00:42:59.754
were really targeted either for
digital commerce or whatever.

00:42:59.754 --> 00:43:01.670
CHRIS CUMMINGS: I would
add one thing to that.

00:43:01.670 --> 00:43:04.720
And that is just LL Bean
is a pretty sophisticated

00:43:04.720 --> 00:43:06.860
digital marketer
in a lot of ways.

00:43:06.860 --> 00:43:10.390
And I think there was a desire
to have some best of breed

00:43:10.390 --> 00:43:13.630
capabilities in a number of
different areas versus kind

00:43:13.630 --> 00:43:18.022
of get inside someone's platform
and just live within there.

00:43:18.022 --> 00:43:20.230
And I don't know, I've seen
those in multiple places.

00:43:20.230 --> 00:43:22.300
Sometimes they've worked well.

00:43:22.300 --> 00:43:25.480
A lot of times, the dream
doesn't come together

00:43:25.480 --> 00:43:27.940
by getting this all
from one vendor.

00:43:27.940 --> 00:43:29.380
But that was at play as well.

00:43:34.979 --> 00:43:35.520
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:43:35.520 --> 00:43:38.733
I hope you understand, my
voice is a little bit--

00:43:38.733 --> 00:43:42.476
but thank you very much.

00:43:42.476 --> 00:43:46.440
As you explained, you
did a really big change

00:43:46.440 --> 00:43:47.830
in your company.

00:43:47.830 --> 00:43:49.146
Who was initializing this?

00:43:49.146 --> 00:43:54.602
Was this a technical part or
was it done by management?

00:43:54.602 --> 00:43:56.400
CHRIS CUMMINGS: Who
here sponsored it?

00:43:59.080 --> 00:44:01.270
RANDY DYER: So I
would say it was both.

00:44:07.060 --> 00:44:13.120
The original thoughts
around our changing customer

00:44:13.120 --> 00:44:15.620
base and those kind of
things and, oh, by the way,

00:44:15.620 --> 00:44:17.650
we're not really
servicing them very well,

00:44:17.650 --> 00:44:27.340
is something that our leadership
had recognized a while ago.

00:44:27.340 --> 00:44:31.066
And so that's kind
of how it started.

00:44:31.066 --> 00:44:33.190
From there, we kind of took
it and said, all right,

00:44:33.190 --> 00:44:39.827
what sort of the things that
are holding us back there?

00:44:39.827 --> 00:44:40.910
And what do we need to do?

00:44:40.910 --> 00:44:42.880
So that's kind of
when it kind of became

00:44:42.880 --> 00:44:49.450
that blend of more business
and technology kinds of things.

00:44:49.450 --> 00:44:54.790
Our sponsor really when
we started this off

00:44:54.790 --> 00:44:59.530
was the senior vice
president of marketing.

00:44:59.530 --> 00:45:04.270
And he was kind of
our initial support.

00:45:04.270 --> 00:45:06.460
From there, though,
we quickly were

00:45:06.460 --> 00:45:10.810
able to get the support of the
CIO and the chief marketing

00:45:10.810 --> 00:45:16.310
officer and all of
really the c-suite there.

00:45:16.310 --> 00:45:16.810
And

00:45:16.810 --> 00:45:18.580
CHRIS CUMMINGS: It
was that one-pager.

00:45:18.580 --> 00:45:20.570
Honestly, it was
that one page that

00:45:20.570 --> 00:45:22.120
brought everybody together.

00:45:22.120 --> 00:45:24.940
It was pretty kind of
mind boggling to me

00:45:24.940 --> 00:45:26.740
that that did what it did.

00:45:26.740 --> 00:45:29.723
But it was worth about a
billion words in this case.

00:45:37.389 --> 00:45:38.430
RANDY DYER: Nothing else?

00:45:38.430 --> 00:45:39.290
Happy hour.

00:45:39.290 --> 00:45:40.420
All right.

00:45:40.420 --> 00:45:41.030
CHRIS CUMMINGS:
Thanks, everybody.

00:45:41.030 --> 00:45:41.946
RANDY DYER: Thank you.

00:45:41.946 --> 00:45:44.980
[MUSIC PLAYING]

